---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Cost Analytics @ GEP — Avyay">
  <article class="case-study" data-chapters>

      <!-- ── Hero ── -->
      <header class="cs-hero">
        <span class="cs-index type-body-sm text-disabled">/01</span>
        <h1 class="type-title">Cost Analytics @ GEP</h1>
        <p class="gep-prose type-body-md text-secondary">Redesigning the user experience for a cost analytics platform to increase usability, adoption and tight integration among sourcing teams.</p>
        <img
          class="cs-hero-img"
          src="/assets/index/case-study-01.png"
          alt="GEP Cost Analytics dashboard"
          width="860"
          height="360"
        />
      </header>

      <!-- ── Project Info ── -->
      <section class="cs-meta-section">
        <div class="cs-meta-grid">
          <div class="cs-meta-item">
            <span class="cs-meta-label type-h4">Role</span>
            <span class="cs-meta-value type-body-sm">UX Designer</span>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-meta-item">
            <span class="cs-meta-label type-h4">Team</span>
            <span class="cs-meta-value type-body-sm">1 Senior UX Designer, 2 Product Managers</span>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-meta-item">
            <span class="cs-meta-label type-h4">Tools</span>
            <span class="cs-meta-value type-body-sm">Figma</span>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-meta-item">
            <span class="cs-meta-label type-h4">Methods</span>
            <span class="cs-meta-value type-body-sm">Proto personas, Jobs To Be Done, Heuristic Evaluation, Journey Mapping, Iterative Prototyping</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ── Overview ── -->
      <section id="overview" class="cs-section" data-chapter>
        <h2 class="gep-section-label type-h4">Overview</h2>
        <p class="cs-lead gep-prose type-body-md text-secondary">Tools should never hinder ability. Ensuring stakeholders have the right tools for cost management is crucial for unlocking maximum business value.</p>
        <div class="cs-two-col">
          <div>
            <h3 class="type-h2">Problem</h3>
            <p class="gep-prose type-body-md">Category Managers & Cost Engineers have a variety of tools at their disposal to estimate costs of sourcing resources. But the learning curve on these tools is high, increasing time required by new users with solid fundamentals to create impact.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="type-h2">Solution</h3>
            <p class="gep-prose type-body-md">Given the freedom to experiment with designs not limited by legacy standards, we introduced design features and redefined flows typically found in consumer-facing applications to reduce the learning curve and increase time spent creating business value.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ── Business Impact ── -->
      <section id="impact" class="cs-section" data-chapter>
        <h2 class="gep-section-label type-h4">Business Impact</h2>
        <div class="cs-impact-grid">
          <div class="cs-impact-card">
            <span class="cs-impact-number">16%</span>
            <span class="type-body-sm text-secondary">Improvement in likability scores</span>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-impact-card">
            <span class="cs-impact-number">500+</span>
            <span class="type-body-sm text-secondary">New cost models created within 2 weeks of launch</span>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-impact-card">
            <span class="cs-impact-number">↑</span>
            <span class="type-body-sm text-secondary">Product stickiness increased</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ── Final Design ── -->
      <section id="final-design" class="cs-section" data-chapter>
        <h2 class="gep-section-label type-h4">Final Application Design</h2>
        <h3 class="cs-statement type-h1">Support cost decisions effectively.</h3>
        <p class="gep-prose type-body-md">Cost Analytics provides a suite of tools that equips stakeholders to identify trends, stay up to date with markets and plan for a variety of scenarios, facilitating organisations to make bold and calculated decisions.</p>
        <ul class="cs-feature-list gep-prose type-body-md">
          <li><strong>Identify market trends</strong> - Source resources cost effectively with the highlighted market trends and maximise margins.</li>
          <li><strong>Stay up to date with Alerts</strong> - Setup alerts to stay up to date and get notified of fluctuations in market indices.</li>
          <li><strong>Plan for adversity</strong> - Prepare for unforeseen scenarios by exploring and visualising variations in cost models with What-if modelling.</li>
          <li><strong>Monitor with an AI-powered dashboard</strong> - Monitor everything important, and receive an AI powered overview of the most consequential fluctuations.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- ── Domain Research ── -->
      <section id="domain-research" class="cs-section" data-chapter>
        <h2 class="gep-section-label type-h4">Domain Research</h2>
        <div class="cs-research-block">
          <h3 class="type-h2">What is Sourcing?</h3>
          <p class="gep-prose type-body-md">Sourcing is the process used to identify, evaluate, and acquire items required by organisations to conduct operations. When an organisation decides to manufacture a new product, they start by understanding the cost opportunities involved. They request tenders from suppliers of the materials, which enables them to figure out who can supply which materials at the best costs.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-research-block">
          <h3 class="type-h2">Where does Cost Analytics fit in?</h3>
          <p class="gep-prose type-body-md">After suppliers submit bids, organisations need to negotiate with them for a good deal. By leveraging market indices and price libraries, and simulating all potential cost drivers, organisations are able to determine what a product <em>should</em> cost, enabling competitive, well-informed negotiations.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ── Stakeholders ── -->
      <section id="stakeholders" class="cs-section" data-chapter>
        <h2 class="gep-section-label type-h4">Understanding the Stakeholders</h2>
        <p class="gep-prose type-body-md">Users for the application were not readily accessible for primary formative research, so we analysed business requirements from clients and had multiple in-depth discussions with members of the product team who had worked closely with users.</p>

        <div class="cs-persona">
          <div class="cs-persona-header">
            <span class="cs-persona-num">01</span>
            <h3 class="type-h2">Category Managers</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="gep-prose type-body-md text-secondary">Source materials for their organisation from suppliers at the best possible price. They monitor category savings, identify cost opportunities, and take decisions on overall purchase strategies.</p>
          <div class="cs-jtbd-list">
            <blockquote class="cs-quote gep-prose type-body-md text-secondary">"When I receive quotes from suppliers, I want to compare the costs with the cost benchmark my cost engineer has created. This allows me to ensure that the costs are negotiated at a fair value."</blockquote>
            <blockquote class="cs-quote gep-prose type-body-md text-secondary">"When markets fluctuate, I need to know which categories are impacted, and I want to understand the trend of the impact so that I can plan my sourcing activities."</blockquote>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-persona">
          <div class="cs-persona-header">
            <span class="cs-persona-num">02</span>
            <h3 class="type-h2">Cost Engineers</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="gep-prose type-body-md text-secondary">Subject matter experts on costs. They digitise the breakdown of costs to be used by category managers when sourcing items, and model alternative scenarios to support decision-making.</p>
          <div class="cs-jtbd-list">
            <blockquote class="cs-quote gep-prose type-body-md text-secondary">"I want to be able to further anticipate potential changes in market conditions and visualise the impact on my expected costs, staying prepared in case of any unexpected developments."</blockquote>
            <blockquote class="cs-quote gep-prose type-body-md text-secondary">"I want to be able to modify cost structures as and when manufacturing processes change, so that stakeholders are always up to date with the should costs of products."</blockquote>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ── Problem Statement ── -->
      <section id="problem-statement" class="cs-section" data-chapter>
        <h2 class="gep-section-label type-h4">Problem Statement</h2>
        <blockquote class="cs-problem-statement type-h1">"How can we enable Cost Engineers to effectively support various Category Managers in their decision making processes?"</blockquote>
        <div class="cs-needs-grid">
          <div class="cs-need">
            <span class="cs-need-num">01</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="type-h3">Vendor Negotiation Tools</strong>
              <p class="type-body-sm">A more integrated approach enabling comprehensive preparation for negotiations.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-need">
            <span class="cs-need-num">02</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="type-h3">Market Trends Alerts</strong>
              <p class="type-body-sm">A robust, omni-present market trends notification system for proactive decisions.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-need">
            <span class="cs-need-num">03</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="type-h3">Contingency Planning</strong>
              <p class="type-body-sm">More versatile tools to seek out and plan for unforeseen contingencies.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-need">
            <span class="cs-need-num">04</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="type-h3">Improved Monitoring & Alignment</strong>
              <p class="type-body-sm">A solution to monitor cost-sensitive factors and keep internal stakeholders aligned.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ── Process ── -->
      <section id="process" class="cs-section" data-chapter>
        <h2 class="gep-section-label type-h4">Repeatable Design Process</h2>
        <p class="gep-prose type-body-md">Once we realised the various features were independent of each other, much like different tools for different tasks, a repeatable process emerged that we used across all five product features.</p>
        <ol class="cs-process-list gep-prose type-body-md">
          <li>Understand user goals through Jobs to be Done</li>
          <li>Identify why the current product makes it challenging to achieve those goals</li>
          <li>Refer to competitors and analogous solutions to mitigate the challenges</li>
          <li>Verify the validity and usability of the design with domain experts</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <!-- ── Learnings ── -->
      <section id="learnings" class="cs-section" data-chapter="Learnings">
        <h2 class="gep-section-label type-h4">Conclusion & Learnings</h2>
        <p class="gep-prose type-body-md">Being in a new field, I learnt to look at ambiguity as an opportunity to differentiate my designs from competitors.</p>
        <div class="cs-learnings">
          <div class="cs-learning">
            <span class="cs-learning-num">01</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="type-h3">Verifying Assumptions</strong>
              <p class="type-body-sm">It saves a lot of time and effort to verify assumptions before designing a solution.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-learning">
            <span class="cs-learning-num">02</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="type-h3">Accepting Ambiguity</strong>
              <p class="type-body-sm">Being comfortable not knowing everything about your user, and revisiting features based on updated understanding, is critical.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-learning">
            <span class="cs-learning-num">03</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="type-h3">Simple… yet Complex</strong>
              <p class="type-body-sm">Creating simple designs is essential. But knowing when adding complexity helps functionality is equally important.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-learning">
            <span class="cs-learning-num">04</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="type-h3">Context</strong>
              <p class="type-body-sm">Understanding the context within which a product is used is key to designing novel, yet familiar experiences.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-learning">
            <span class="cs-learning-num">05</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="type-h3">Differentiation</strong>
              <p class="type-body-sm">Finding the one singular factor that defines the problem is of utmost importance, and tackling this elevates the experience.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .case-study {
    max-width: 860px;
    padding: 80px clamp(40px, 8vw, 100px) 120px;
  }

  /* Long-form running text. The body primitives are set at line-height 1.3,
     which suits the UI-length copy they were drawn for; a case study
     paragraph runs ten lines, so it relaxes the leading and holds a measure.
     Family, size and colour still come from .type-body-md. */
  .gep-prose {
    max-width: 680px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  /* ── Hero ── */
  .cs-hero {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 16px;
    margin-bottom: 72px;
  }

  .cs-index {
    display: block;
  }

  /* Matches the card and visual radius in the primitives */
  .cs-hero-img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 360px;
    object-fit: cover;
    border-radius: 12px;
    margin-top: 8px;
  }

  /* ── Sections ──
     The page's own rhythm, matching Moody's and About: a rule, then a quiet
     uppercase label, then the section's content. The project meta sits with
     the hero rather than as a section of its own, so it carries no rule. */
  .cs-section {
    margin-top: 56px;
    padding-top: 48px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .gep-section-label {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 32px;
  }

  .cs-statement {
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .cs-lead {
    margin-bottom: 32px;
  }

  /* ── Project meta ── */
  .cs-meta-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 24px 48px;
  }

  .cs-meta-item {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 4px;
  }

  /* A meta value is a fact rather than running text, so it keeps body-sm's
     family and size but takes the full-strength text colour */
  .cs-meta-value {
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--color-text-strong);
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* ── Two col ── */
  .cs-two-col {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 40px;
  }

  /* ── Impact ── */
  .cs-impact-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 2px;
    background: var(--color-divider);
    border-radius: 12px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* Static card surface, same cool fill the About and Moody's cards use */
  .cs-impact-card {
    background: var(--color-cool-bg);
    padding: 32px 28px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  /* The one deliberate typographic deviation on this page: the primitives
     stop at .type-title (36px) and have no display numeral, and an impact
     figure needs to read as a headline number rather than a heading. So it
     stays a local style, but on the primitives' own axes: the heading serif,
     one step above .type-h1, in the accent text token. */
  .cs-impact-number {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 48px;
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--color-text-accent);
  }

  /* ── Features ── */
  .cs-feature-list {
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-top: 24px;
  }

  .cs-feature-list li {
    padding-left: 20px;
    position: relative;
  }

  .cs-feature-list li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 12px;
    width: 8px;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--color-text-accent);
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  /* ── Research ── */
  .cs-research-block {
    margin-bottom: 32px;
  }

  .cs-research-block h3 {
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  /* ── Personas ── */
  /* Static card surface: the cool fill carries it in both themes, with no
     hover state and no border, the same way the About cards read. */
  .cs-persona {
    margin-top: 40px;
    padding: 32px;
    background: var(--color-cool-bg);
    border-radius: 12px;
  }

  .cs-persona-header {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  /* The step/index numerals shared by personas, needs, learnings and the
     process list. Grouped so the four stay in step. They sit below the
     primitives' smallest size and one of the four is a ::before, so they
     stay local; only the colour comes from the accent text token. */
  .cs-persona-num,
  .cs-need-num,
  .cs-learning-num,
  .cs-process-list li::before {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 10px;
    color: var(--color-text-accent);
  }

  .cs-persona-num {
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .cs-jtbd-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-top: 20px;
  }

  /* Sits inside the persona card, so it takes the page surface to separate
     itself from the cool fill behind it */
  .cs-quote {
    border: none;
    padding: 12px 16px;
    background: var(--color-bg-default);
    border-radius: 12px;
    font-style: italic;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* ── Problem statement ──
     Type comes from .type-h1; this only holds the measure and the spacing,
     and undoes the browser's blockquote chrome. */
  .cs-problem-statement {
    max-width: 680px;
    margin-bottom: 40px;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
  }

  /* ── Needs ── */
  .cs-needs-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 24px;
  }

  .cs-need {
    display: flex;
    gap: 16px;
    align-items: flex-start;
  }

  .cs-need-num {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-top: 5px;
  }

  /* ── Process ── */
  .cs-process-list {
    list-style: none;
    counter-reset: process;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 16px;
    margin-top: 24px;
  }

  .cs-process-list li {
    counter-increment: process;
    display: flex;
    gap: 16px;
    align-items: flex-start;
  }

  .cs-process-list li::before {
    content: "0" counter(process);
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-top: 5px;
  }

  /* ── Learnings ── */
  .cs-learnings {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 28px;
    margin-top: 32px;
  }

  .cs-learning {
    display: flex;
    gap: 20px;
    align-items: flex-start;
  }

  .cs-learning-num {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-top: 5px;
  }

  /* The card titles in the needs and learnings grids carry .type-h3; these
     only make them own their line and clear the copy beneath. */
  .cs-need strong,
  .cs-learning strong {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }

</style>
